WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:04.713
There are at least
56 million kilometers

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:06.840
standing between
us and Mars.

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:11.470
That’s a long
way to travel.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.933
♪♪

00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:19.228
But what if there 
was something

00:00:19.228 --> 00:00:20.229
that could help?

00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:22.940
An untapped force,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.650
all around us.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.777
That could give
us an extra push.

00:00:28.111 --> 00:00:29.863
I'm Wrenn Schmidt and we're
exploring the science

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.615
behind "For All Mankind."

00:00:37.204 --> 00:00:38.121
With a journey to Mars

00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:39.665
heating up between NASA,

00:00:39.665 --> 00:00:41.625
Helios and the Soviet Union

00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:43.335
we wanted to 
create a thrilling

00:00:43.335 --> 00:00:45.045
come from behind surprise.

00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:47.464
What could give 
someone an unexpected

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:50.550
edge in this
literal space race?

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:55.055
The answer led us
into the light.

00:00:57.266 --> 00:00:58.976
Light is made up of photons.

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:02.396
Photons don’t have any mass.

00:01:03.981 --> 00:01:05.565
But they do have momentum.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.902
A single photon
soaring through space

00:01:10.362 --> 00:01:11.405
can't do much.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.492
But, the sun
pumps out trillions

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.077
of photons
per second.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.915
And all these photons
in zero gravity

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.083
well, they can
pack a punch.

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.501
(ping pong balls dropping)

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:25.627
♪♪

00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:32.301
So...

00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:33.510
how can we
harness them?

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:41.935
We need something
large and reflective.

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:44.187
Something that can
bounce light.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:48.734
A solar sail made of
Mylar can do just that.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:53.947
♪♪

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.742
The photons bouce off the
reflective sail,

00:01:56.742 --> 00:01:58.493
transferring their momentum,

00:01:58.493 --> 00:02:00.245
pushing the ship forward.

00:02:00.746 --> 00:02:02.998
More photons means
more momentum.

00:02:03.749 --> 00:02:05.876
The impact may be 
small, but it's steady.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.171
It’s a little boost that
can really add up.

00:02:09.755 --> 00:02:11.882
To push a ship as
big as NASA's Sojourner

00:02:11.882 --> 00:02:14.217
you need a
really big sail.

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:16.470
Making a sail that big
would be challenging

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.554
and the bigger the sail,

00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:19.556
the more fuel you'd
need to launch.

00:02:20.474 --> 00:02:22.476
But a ship with a
large enough sail

00:02:22.476 --> 00:02:25.979
could ride the sunlight
all the way to Mars.

00:02:27.731 --> 00:02:29.274
Riding Photons
through space

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:31.151
may sound like fiction,

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:32.611
but it's science.

00:02:32.611 --> 00:02:35.155
Scientists are currently
exploring how the use

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:37.366
of lasers and thinner,
lighter materials

00:02:37.366 --> 00:02:39.368
could open up new
possibilities.

00:02:39.368 --> 00:02:42.788
Even now there is a
toaster-sized space craft

00:02:42.788 --> 00:02:45.374
orbiting the Earth
by solar sail.

00:02:45.374 --> 00:02:47.125
Well, solar sails that
could propel a ship

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:48.335
as massive as
Sojourner

00:02:48.335 --> 00:02:49.920
aren't reality
just yet.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.172
A future of
space exploration

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:54.174
fueled by the Sun is certainly

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:55.467
a powerful idea.

